       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Sertraline 1000 mg is five times the 200 mg/day maximum. I can't think of a clinical situation where that dose is appropriate for a 70 kg adult with normal kidney and liver function. It could cause serious harm, including serotonin syndrome, seizures, arrhythmia, and death. Your confirmation doesn't change that, because the problem is the dose itself.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** Tell me the dose (for example 50 mg or 100 mg once daily) and I'll enter it now.\n- **Talk to your resident or attending.** If they want something unusual, they should review it and enter it themselves. Pharmacy will probably flag and intervene on this dose anyway.\n- **Check for a mix-up.** Make sure the dose wasn't meant for a different drug, or that the unit or decimal place isn't wrong.\n\nIf you're worried the patient has already taken too much sertraline, call Poison Control (1-800-222-1222 in the US) and tell your team right away."
